import{Q as e,X as t,ft as n,n as r}from"./chunks/framework.ogKpVNID.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Node Compatibility","description":"","frontmatter":{"id":"guide.node-compatibility","title":"Node Compatibility","describes":["proposal.0001"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/node-compatibility.md","filePath":"guides/node-compatibility.md"}`),a={name:`guides/node-compatibility.md`};function o(r,i,a,o,s,c){return n(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="node-compatibility" tabindex="-1">Node Compatibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#node-compatibility" aria-label="Permalink to “Node Compatibility”">​</a></h1><h2 id="what-it-does" tabindex="-1">What it does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-does" aria-label="Permalink to “What it does”">​</a></h2><p>Bento executes installed JavaScript applications in-process using V8 and Deno-derived Node and Web compatibility. The milestone targets the locked Remix 3 guest-book dependency graph, not every Node 26 API or arbitrary npm package.</p><p>The exercised Node surface includes <code>node:path</code>, <code>node:fs</code>, <code>node:fs/promises</code>, <code>node:sqlite</code>, and streaming <code>node:http</code> servers. SQLite supports migrations, parameterized statements, persistent databases, and explicit connection cleanup. Servers support <code>close()</code> and <code>closeAllConnections()</code>.</p><p>Available globals include <code>process.argv</code>, instance-local <code>process.cwd()</code> and <code>process.env</code>, <code>Buffer</code>, <code>import.meta.dirname</code> and <code>import.meta.filename</code>, <code>createRequire</code>, microtasks, structured cloning, abort controllers, <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code>, Fetch requests and responses, form data, and readable streams.</p><h2 id="module-resolution" tabindex="-1">Module resolution <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-resolution" aria-label="Permalink to “Module resolution”">​</a></h2><p>JavaScript entries and dependencies can use <code>.js</code>, <code>.mjs</code>, and <code>.cjs</code>. CommonJS modules imported by ESM receive generated facades with statically discovered named exports; CommonJS <code>require()</code> uses the upstream Node compatibility resolver. The runtime&#39;s ESM resolver uses Oxc with Node&#39;s <code>node</code>, <code>import</code>, and <code>default</code> conditions, package <code>exports</code> and <code>imports</code>, and real-path pnpm symlink resolution. Relative ESM imports must name the complete file, including its extension. TypeScript <code>paths</code> and <code>baseUrl</code> do not change runtime resolution.</p><p>Install the project&#39;s locked dependencies before execution. A missing package reports the specifier and recommends <code>bento install</code>; application startup never invokes an installer or downloads dependencies. An unregistered <code>host:</code> import reports that the host did not register the module, without an installation suggestion. Remote URL imports and guest subprocess execution are unavailable regardless of network grants. Installation and explicitly trusted project tasks are separate host operations.</p><p>Bento transforms <code>.ts</code>, <code>.mts</code>, <code>.cts</code>, <code>.tsx</code>, and <code>.jsx</code> in memory through <code>bento_compiler</code>; plain JavaScript passes through unchanged. Explicit TypeScript extensions in imports work. The project-root <code>tsconfig.json</code> and its inherited execution settings are loaded once per instance. Installed files beneath the canonical project root&#39;s <code>node_modules</code> use default compiler settings rather than inheriting the application&#39;s settings. A project whose own root has a <code>node_modules</code> ancestor still uses its project settings. <code>noEmit</code> never disables runtime transformation, and no emitted JavaScript is written to disk.</p><p>Configuration diagnostics print once per instance at startup to the host process&#39;s stderr. Unsupported JSX execution settings such as <code>jsx: preserve</code> and <code>react-native</code> allow non-JSX modules to run; only modules containing JSX fail at transform time as <code>Transform</code> errors naming the setting and the JSX location. An unreadable or malformed <code>tsconfig.json</code> returns a <code>Transform</code> error from <code>start</code> before execution. Source maps preserve original TypeScript line and column positions in exceptions. Transformation does not perform semantic type checking.</p><h2 id="grants-and-native-code" tabindex="-1">Grants and native code <a class="header-anchor" href="#grants-and-native-code" aria-label="Permalink to “Grants and native code”">​</a></h2><p>Hosts supply separate read, write, network, environment, and native-addon grants. Ungranted operations fail without prompting. Filesystem checks resolve symlinks before checking scoped roots, so a link inside an allowed directory does not authorize access to an unrelated directory. Environment reads outside the environment grant return <code>undefined</code>; enumeration excludes them, and writes fail. Guest environment changes affect only that runtime&#39;s map, never the embedding process environment.</p><p>Every instance may read the process&#39;s user and group IDs through <code>process.getuid()</code> and <code>process.getgid()</code>, because Node&#39;s <code>fs.access</code> needs them to evaluate a file&#39;s permission bits. No other system information is available, and no grant enables it: <code>os.hostname()</code>, <code>os.userInfo()</code>, <code>os.homedir()</code>, <code>os.networkInterfaces()</code>, <code>os.cpus()</code>, and the memory, load, uptime, and OS release queries fail with <code>NotCapable</code>. The platform and architecture Bento was built for remain visible through <code>process.platform</code> and <code>process.arch</code>.</p><p>The locked <code>oxc-parser</code>, <code>oxc-resolver</code>, <code>oxc-transform</code>, <code>oxc-minify</code>, and <code>lightningcss</code> packages use their actual Node-API binaries. Addon authority must cover the native binary&#39;s real path before initialization. Granting the project&#39;s <code>node_modules</code> root covers its pnpm virtual store. Loading denied addons does not initialize them; the diagnostic identifies the package or guest call site and names the host&#39;s <code>addons</code> grant (<code>--allow-addons</code> in the CLI).</p><p>Native-loading executables must export the Node-API ABI symbols. With the matching <code>deno_napi = &quot;=0.190.0&quot;</code> build dependency, call <code>deno_napi::print_linker_flags(&quot;your-binary-name&quot;)</code> from the executable&#39;s <code>build.rs</code>. Linking the runtime alone does not prevent the linker from stripping addon symbols.</p><p>Remix asset discovery probes <code>&lt;ancestor&gt;/node_modules</code> for optional dependencies all the way to the filesystem root. A read grant covering only the project root fails with a <code>Permission</code> error pointing into the asset code, even when the probed dependency does not exist. For scoped reads, grant the canonical project root plus every path returned by <code>bento_runtime::package_lookup_paths(&amp;root)?</code>. This helper includes the project&#39;s own <code>node_modules</code>, resolves symlinks, and preserves nonexistent path tails; it returns an I/O error if a path cannot be canonicalized. Hosts choose whether to add these paths. Bento never broadens read grants or disguises permission denials as missing files.</p><p>The fixture&#39;s CLI instructions use explicit unrestricted reads for convenience. Embedded hosts can instead scope reads to the project and trusted package-lookup roots, writes to the database directory, and addon loading to the project&#39;s <code>node_modules</code>. Inspect canonical lookup paths before granting them: an ancestor&#39;s <code>node_modules</code> symlink grants reads throughout its target tree. A project beneath an ancestor writable by other users, such as a shared temporary directory, requires particular care.</p><p>An authorized addon executes native code inside the Rust host process. It is not contained by JavaScript filesystem or network permissions. Only enable trusted native packages; these grants are application policy, not a hostile-code sandbox. The real Remix asset orchestration still controls development source maps and stable URLs, and production minification and fingerprinting.</p><p>The native Oxc resolver also reads the embedding process&#39;s <code>NODE_PATH</code>. If that variable points at another project&#39;s packages, asset resolution can leave the installed fixture graph and a scoped read grant will reject the result. Unset host-process <code>NODE_PATH</code> before launching when resolution must stay within the prepared project. Guest environment grants do not constrain native addon reads of the host environment, and Bento does not mutate the embedding process&#39;s environment to hide them.</p><h2 id="node-compatibility-decisions" tabindex="-1">Node compatibility decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#node-compatibility-decisions" aria-label="Permalink to “Node compatibility decisions”">​</a></h2><p><code>EventTarget.addEventListener(type, listener, { signal })</code> accepts Node-style signal objects with an <code>aborted</code> property. Already-aborted signals prevent registration, and an <code>abort</code> event removes the listener. Native <code>AbortSignal</code> objects retain their ordinary behavior. Remix 3&#39;s server renderer relies on this duck-typed signal behavior; strict branded-signal validation would break rendering.</p><p><code>server.listen(port)</code> with no host listens on <code>::</code>, accepting IPv4 and IPv6 connections when the system supports dual-stack IPv6. IPv4-only systems fall back to <code>0.0.0.0</code>. An omitted host does not mean loopback-only; grant and deployment policy must account for the listening address.</p><h2 id="observable-failures" tabindex="-1">Observable failures <a class="header-anchor" href="#observable-failures" aria-label="Permalink to “Observable failures”">​</a></h2><p>Runtime outcomes distinguish completed execution, guest exits, failures, and host-requested shutdown. Errors identify startup or syntax failures, module resolution failures, transformation failures, permission violations, and uncaught exceptions or unhandled rejections. Diagnostics include the entry or failing source location instead of presenting a failed launch as successful execution. Guest <code>process.exit</code> disposes only the runtime, including inside HTTP handlers; it never exits the Rust embedding process.</p><p><code>RuntimeError::location()</code> returns an optional <code>SourceLocation { path, line, column }</code> with one-based original-source positions. Transform errors use the compiler diagnostic; uncaught errors and unhandled rejections use the first local project stack frame; resolution errors identify the importing module and, when available, the import literal. Environment-file contents are never used as diagnostic source locations. <code>RuntimeError.message</code> is plain text without ANSI escape sequences regardless of terminal color settings; the host or CLI chooses presentation. Unterminated terminal sequences in guest messages do not hide subsequent stack frames or causes, and ordinary tabs and line breaks are preserved. A new <code>Error</code> copying a caught module failure&#39;s message remains <code>Uncaught</code>, including when the text is identical. Guest <code>SyntaxError</code> exceptions, such as a failed <code>JSON.parse</code>, are also <code>Uncaught</code>; source parsing failures are <code>Startup</code>.</p><p>Permission errors follow nested causes to the denied operation&#39;s local source frame, including installed package files. A failure wrapped by the asset pipeline therefore identifies the asset code that attempted the read rather than losing the denial in a generic compilation error.</p><p>Subprocess entrypoints such as <code>spawn</code> and <code>execSync</code> throw <code>NotCapable</code> before execution. This explicit denial preserves permission diagnostics rather than wrapping a denied command as a generic unsuccessful child exit. The native subprocess operations also remain ungranted, including access through lower-level compatibility APIs.</p><p>Each instance owns its permissions, environment, and execution thread. Concurrent instances do not inherit each other&#39;s grants. The Rust host drives execution directly; no Node, Bun, Deno, or pnpm process executes the application.</p><p>Signals are virtual inside embedded instances: registering a guest <code>process.on(&quot;SIGTERM&quot;)</code>, <code>process.on(&quot;SIGINT&quot;)</code>, or other supported signal listener never installs an OS signal handler. The embedding host explicitly delivers interrupt or termination through <code>RuntimeHandle</code>; the CLI owns process-global OS signal forwarding. Shutdown waits for cooperative guest cleanup up to a five-second default deadline, then interrupts JavaScript and disposes the instance. A deadline interruption is never reported as graceful. Listeners and active connections are released before completed shutdown returns.</p><p>See <a href="./embedding-bento">Embedding Bento</a> for Rust configuration, asynchronous private host modules, instance isolation, execution outcomes, and shutdown control. Authorized native addons and arbitrary blocking native code are outside the JavaScript containment and interruption guarantees.</p>`,30)]])}var s=r(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};